    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-s/q4-question-1.svg\" alt=\"Two candidates, A and B , are about to be interviewed by a company. The probability that both candidates will be unsuccessful is the same as the probability that candidate B will be unsuccessful. Show that the probability that both candidates will be successful is the same as the probability that candidate A will be successful. You may not assume independence in this question. ( ) 4\" style=\"--madas-width:573.3px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-s/q4-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Let A, B denote success: [M1] The assumption says P(A′ ∩B′) = P(B′). [B1] Since B′ splits into A ∩B′ and A′ ∩B′, it follows that P(A ∩B′) = 0. [A1] In the source Venn diagram the A-only region consequently has zero probability. All the probability of A is contained within B.\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-s/q4-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Thus P(A) = P(A ∩B) + P(A ∩B′) = P(A ∩B) , as required: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:438.7px\"></figure>",
