    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-s/q8",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-s/q8-question-1.svg\" alt=\"1   1   2   3   4   5   6   6\" style=\"--madas-width:454.7px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-s/q8-question-2.svg\" alt=\"The figure below shows a contraption where balls are rolling down a slope and fall into one of 8 vertical tubes. There is equal chance of a ball falling into any of these 8 vertical tubes. The ball scores according to which tube it falls in, and these scores are marked clearly in the figure below. Two balls are rolled in succession. Determine the probability that … a) … their sum is even. ( ) 3 ( ) 4 b) … the second ball will score higher than the first. c) … their sum is even and the second ball will score higher than the first. ( ) 5\" style=\"--madas-width:568.0px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-s/q8-question-3.svg\" alt=\"[continues overleaf]\" style=\"--madas-width:558.7px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-s/q8-question-4.svg\" alt=\"[continued from overleaf] Three balls are rolled in succession. Determine the probability that … ( ) 3 d) … they will fall into different tubes. e) … their scores will be different. ( ) 8\" style=\"--madas-width:574.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-s/q8-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"The multiplicities for scores 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 are 2, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2. (a) There are four tubes for each parity: [M1] Equal parity has probability (4/8)2 + (4/8)2 = 1/2 . [M1] [A1] (b) Grouping by the second score 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, the source counts respectively 2(6), 1(5), 1(4), 1(3), 1(2) favourable ordered tube pairs: Their sum is 12 + 5 + 4 + 3 + 2 = 26. Alternatively use symmetry: Of 64 ordered pairs of tubes, 22 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 22 = 12 [B1] have equal scores: [A1] The other 52 split equally between the two orders, so the probability is 26/64 = 13/32 . [M1] [A1] (c) To enumerate the increasing same-parity scores, the odd pairs (1, 3), (1, 5), (3, 5) contribute 2 + 2 + 1 = 5 ordered tube pairs:\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-s/q8-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"The even pairs (2, 4), (2, 6), (4, 6) contribute 1 + 2 + 2 = 5. Of the 32 equal-parity pairs, the same 12 have equal scores: [B1] [A1] Half the remaining 20 have increasing scores, giving 10/64 = 5/32 . [M1] [M1] [A1] (d) The second and third rolls must avoid the tubes already visited, giving (7/8)(6/8) = 21/32 . [M1] [M1] [A1] (e) The handwritten method counts the complement: There are 83 = 512 ordered tube triples: The labels 1 and 6 have two tubes apiece; labels 2 to 5 have one apiece: The factor 3 in the first five rows places the unequal roll: Repeated score(s) and odd score Count Calculation 3(22)(4) Two 1s, odd score 2 to 5 48 Two equal from 2 to 5, odd from other singles 36 3(4)(3) Two 6s, odd score 2 to 5 48 3(22)(4) 3(2)(22)(2) Two 1s and a 6, or two 6s and a 1 48 Two equal from 2 to 5, odd score 1 or 6 48 3(4)(2 + 2) 2(23) Three 1s or three 6s 16 Three equal from 2 to 5 4 4(13)\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-s/q8-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"The repeated-score cases total 48 + 36 + 48 + 48 + 48 + 16 + 4 = 248, so all three scores differ with probability 1 −248/512 = 264/512 . = 33/64  = 56 [B1] [A1] choices of three distinct tubes, six 8 A direct count gives the same result: Of the 3 include both tubes marked 1 and six include both tubes marked 6. [A1] These cases cannot overlap among only three tubes: [A1] Thus there are 56 −12 = 44 [A1] unordered choices with distinct scores, each in six possible orders: [A1] Alternatively count triples by how many scores are drawn from the doubled labels 1 and 6.  = 4 choices; with one there are 2(2)  = 24; with both there are 4 4 With neither there are 3 2 2(2)(4) = 16. There are 4 + 24 + 16 = 44 before ordering: The probability is 44(6)/83 = 33/64 . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:680.0px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 8 is 23 marks.</p>"
    ],
