    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-s/q11",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-s/q11-question-1.svg\" alt=\"A child is sitting over the shoulders of his father whose mass is 100 kg . The father, with the child over his shoulders, is standing in a lift of mass 900 kg . When the tension in the cable of the lift is 10200 N , the lift and its occupants is accelerating, with the child exerting a force of 200 N on the shoulders of his father. Find the magnitude of the force exerted by floor of the lift to the feet of the father. ( ) 9\" style=\"--madas-width:573.3px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-s/q11-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Consider the lift and father together, excluding the child: [B1] [M1] [A1] With upward acceleration a, Newton’s second law gives\" style=\"--madas-width:661.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-s/q11-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"10200 −200 −1000g = 1000a, a = 0.2 m s−2. [M1] [M1] [A1] Equivalently, include the child in the whole-lift balance: If the child’s mass is c, then 200 −cg = ca. Subtracting that equation from the whole-system equation cancels the unknown child mass, yielding the displayed balance: For the father alone the upward normal force R balances his weight and the child’s downward force with the required resultant: R −100g −200 = 100(0.2), R = 1200 N . [M1] [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
